=== P18.6 -- AN OPEN REGRESSION-INTEGRITY CONCERN, recorded when noticed ===
date: 2026-10-01T06:20:53Z

WHAT HAPPENED. include/cfd/species/HeatRelease.hpp was edited AFTER the regression's
release_cuda configuration had already configured, built and passed its staleness check.
The edit added a comment block naming the P15 assembler that consumes S_E -- made because the
integrity probe correctly reported itself BLIND, having grepped for a symbol the header did
not mention.

WHY IT MATTERS. CLAUDE.md section 8: a regression result is authoritative ONLY if the test
binary was demonstrably rebuilt against the exact source being tested. release_cuda's
binaries now predate the current header, so strictly its counts are against a different
source file than the tree's final state.

WHY IT PROBABLY DOES NOT CHANGE THE RESULT -- and 'probably' is why this is recorded as OPEN
rather than dismissed: the edit is a COMMENT BLOCK only. Comments do not reach codegen. But
the source HASH changed, and a hash change is what the staleness check exists to catch, so
the claim needs proving rather than asserting.

HOW IT WILL BE RESOLVED AT CLOSE, in order:
  1. let the regression finish -- build/qual-final must NOT be touched while its ctest reads
     binaries from it;
  2. rebuild build/qual-final and compare libcfdcore.a's sha256 against the value the
     regression recorded at its start. The host toolchain is bit-reproducible, so an
     IDENTICAL hash proves the comment-only edit changed no object code and the release_cuda
     counts stand for the final source;
  3. if the hash DIFFERS, re-run the release_cuda configuration in full. The counts from the
     first run would then be marked INVALID AS AUTHORITATIVE in place, not deleted.

cpu_only and debug_gui are unaffected: both configure and build AFTER this point in the run,
so they compile the edited header and their staleness checks cover it.

